<!--CLI Documentation — UpdateCommand
  FQN: com.machineblocks.bml.cli.documentation.UpdateCommand-->
<clidoc:UpdateCommand xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:clidoc="com.machineblocks.bml.cli.documentation">

  <baseType>
    core:Documentation
  </baseType>
  <is>
    machineblocks update specification
  </is>
  <documentation>
    <![CDATA[
    Syntax:
        machineblocks update [--no-install]

        The command parses flags and rewrites package.json, then prints its
        lines. An unexpected positional argument exits 2 and also prints the
        short usage. An unknown option exits 2 and prints the short usage.

        A project is BlockML when package.json has a blockml key. A missing
        package.json, a package.json without that key, or an unreadable
        package.json exits 2 and writes nothing.

        Does not call updateProject from @blockml/cli. Version source is this
        host's own package.json, the same source init uses when it first adds
        the MachineBlocks pins. @machineblocks/cli becomes ^ plus this host's
        version. @machineblocks/core, @blockml/cli, and @blockml/core become
        the ranges this host declares in dependencies.

        Each pin is updated in the dependency field that already holds it
        (dependencies, devDependencies, peerDependencies, optionalDependencies).
        A missing @machineblocks/cli or @blockml/cli is added to devDependencies.
        A missing @machineblocks/core or @blockml/core is added to dependencies.
        A specifier that contains ":" (workspace:, file:, npm:, git, http, and
        the like) stays as authored. When every registry range already matches,
        package.json is left byte for byte and the result is code 0.

        Unless --no-install, runs npm install in the project root after a
        successful check. npm install still runs when the ranges were already
        current. --no-install skips that install and, when package.json changed,
        prints Next: npm install. Writes package.json only. Leaves BML files,
        AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md unchanged. Does not load blockml.config.json.

        Exit 0 when the ranges are written or already match, including when
        npm install succeeds. Exit 1 when package.json cannot be written or
        npm install fails. Exit 2 on usage, a missing or unreadable package.json,
        a package.json without a blockml key, a dependency field that cannot
        hold a pin, or a missing dependency range in this host's package.json.

    Printed help:
    Usage: machineblocks update [--no-install]
           machineblocks update --help

    When: point an existing BlockML project at the versions of this CLI.

    Rewrites @machineblocks/cli, @machineblocks/core, @blockml/cli, and @blockml/core
    in package.json, then runs npm install.
    A project is BlockML when package.json has a blockml key. Otherwise exit 2
    and nothing is written.

    @machineblocks/cli becomes ^<this CLI version>. @machineblocks/core, @blockml/cli,
    and @blockml/core become the ranges this CLI depends on. A missing
    @machineblocks/cli or @blockml/cli is added in devDependencies. A missing
    @machineblocks/core or @blockml/core is added in dependencies. An entry that
    already lives in another dependency field is updated there. A specifier that
    contains ":" (workspace:, file:, npm:, git, http, …) stays as authored.

    Writes package.json only. BML files, AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md stay as they are.
    --no-install skips npm install. Ranges that already match exit 0.
    npm install still runs in that case unless --no-install.

    Run with npx @machineblocks/cli update so the invoked CLI is the version source.

    Related: init.
    ]]>
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="clidoc:Commands" relation="SeeAlso" />
    <see type="clidoc:InitCommand" relation="SeeAlso" />
  </references>
</clidoc:UpdateCommand>
